From 0c677e84ce621ab4c52616b62ebbffe3cd3ebb50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: linux-a9 <linux-a9@localhost>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 19:48:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: add A9 SEP mailbox and sep-bio transport

The SEP on s8000/s8003 is an ASC coprocessor at 0x20da00000 with a 32-bit
mailbox at +0x4000. Add a driver that owns the mailbox and implements the
endpoint transport recovered from the iOS 16.7.10 kernelcache and a live
os_log capture:

  - 64-bit messages (ep/tag/opcode/param/data), drained and logged;
  - per-endpoint 16 KiB OOL buffers published with control opcodes
    0x04/0x02 (send size/address) and 0x05/0x03 (receive);
  - the transfer framing: tag 0xfc request, command in the opcode field,
    total length in the data field, completion matched on tag and command;
  - cache maintenance around the buffers (dcache clean/inval by VA).

Large transfers above 16356 bytes need the 0xfc/0xfd/0xfe chunk sequence
seen on the wire; that is documented in docs/sep/driver-notes.md and not
implemented yet.

Userspace is the Aurora Silicon ABI (/dev/sep-bio, interface v4) so their
libfprint driver, fprintd and Omarchy's fingerprint UI can be reused
unchanged. Enrol/verify return -ENOSYS until SBIO is implemented and
GET_INFO reports sensor_present = 0 so no unusable reader is exposed.

The mailbox is polled (no SEP node in the DT yet) and stays untouched until
debugfs start is written, because the first read of a powered-down SEP block
can stall the AP. The driver is inert unless sep_a9.enable=1.

When a device tree node (apple,s8003-sep / apple,s8000-sep) is present the
driver binds from OF instead of registering its own platform device, so the
nextint DT can describe the SEP once without a duplicate instance.

Add the j71t/j72s/iPad5 DT node (apple,s8003-sep, mailbox at 0x20da00000,
AIC interrupts 99/98/101/100) so the driver binds from OF.

Implement the SBIO handshake (command 0x73, u32 1) and expose it through
/dev/sep-bio: GET_INFO reports sensor_present only after it succeeds, and
ENROL_START/VERIFY_START send provisional 48/68-byte zeroed payloads with the
caller's flags. Those payload layouts are produced by biometrickitd in
userspace and are not yet recovered, so the commands exercise the transport
without claiming correct semantics.
---
